{"status":"completed","summary":"Configured node1 so only root and opsadmin may use crontab. Created the missing locked opsadmin account, installed /etc/cron.allow with root-only permissions, and verified the policy persists across a node restart.","actions_performed":["Created the local opsadmin account with its password locked.","Installed /etc/cron.allow containing exactly root and opsadmin, owned by root:root with mode 0600.","Verified root and opsadmin are accepted by crontab while nobody is explicitly denied.","Safely restarted node1 and confirmed its boot identity changed.","Verified crond, the account, policy file, and effective access rules recovered after restart."],"evidence":[{"requirement":"Only root and opsadmin may schedule cron jobs on node1","command_ids":["cmd-3b1f83c6e19f4c65","cmd-50a63264dbb24215","cmd-76983b90ae334316"],"summary":"/etc/cron.allow contains exactly root and opsadmin with root:root ownership and mode 0600. Effective checks show root and opsadmin reach their empty crontabs, while nobody receives an explicit access-denied response, both before and after restart."},{"requirement":"node1 restart and recovery","command_ids":["cmd-7c3edd7b04a14e61","cmd-50a63264dbb24215","cmd-58edb8ffe94f4fb1","cmd-c8db0bf7002f485b","cmd-76983b90ae334316"],"summary":"Baseline boot ID was 9bdcc129-3d25-48ff-ad64-56be332fb91e with crond healthy. The safe reboot was acknowledged, and the post-restart boot ID changed to e0c3b037-bc49-48b9-ab9e-5b4416450ea4. After recovery, crond was active/enabled and the account, policy, and effective access behavior remained intact."}]}
